cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "interleavekit: block, convolutional, helical and S-random interleavers with burst-dispersion metrics and latency/memory cost accounting"
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-10-06
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  Four interleaver constructions collected behind one permutation convention --
  a row-write column-read block interleaver with a closed-form minimum spread, a
  shift-register-bank convolutional interleaver with exact per-symbol delays, a
  wrapping-diagonal helical interleaver, and an S-random interleaver built by a
  deterministic backjumping search -- together with the metrics a fading-channel
  designer needs to choose between them: minimum spread, the S-parameter,
  normalised dispersion, and the burst-dispersion family, which answers how long
  a run of consecutive source symbols survives a channel burst of a given length.
  Burst dispersion is computed from the identity that R consecutive source symbols
  can be damaged by a burst of L exactly when the narrowest transmitted window
  holding them is at most L-1 wide, which makes it linear rather than quadratic in
  the block length and is checked against the definitional window scan on 5304
  comparisons. Each construction reports its latency in symbols and milliseconds
  and its memory in symbols and bytes under a stated cost model, and a search
  returns the cheapest parameter set in each family that measurably disperses a
  target burst. Validation level 2, research grade. Not flight-qualified, not
  certified, not approved for operational aerospace use.
authors:
  - name: "OPTIMA Organisation"
keywords:
  - interleaver
  - deinterleaver
  - burst-errors
  - convolutional-interleaver
  - s-random
  - fading-channel
  - error-control-coding
references:
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Ramsey
        given-names: "J. L."
    title: "Realization of Optimum Interleavers"
    journal: "IEEE Transactions on Information Theory"
    volume: 16
    issue: 3
    start: 338
    end: 345
    month: 5
    year: 1970
    notes: >-
      Standard reference for interleaver structures. The bibliographic details
      above were read from the Communications Blockset bibliography on
      2026-10-06; the primary article was not consulted for this build and no
      numerical result or specific claim is attributed to it.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Forney
        given-names: "G. D."
        name-suffix: Jr.
    title: "Burst-Correcting Codes for the Classic Bursty Channel"
    journal: "IEEE Transactions on Communications"
    volume: 19
    start: 772
    end: 781
    month: 10
    year: 1971
    notes: >-
      Standard reference for the convolutional interleaver. Bibliographic details
      read from the Communications Blockset bibliography on 2026-10-06; the
      primary article was not consulted and no numerical result is attributed to
      it. The delay and memory expressions used in this package are derived in
      src/interleavekit/convolutional.py and verified by running the construction.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Clark
        given-names: "George C."
        name-suffix: Jr.
      - family-names: Cain
        given-names: "J. Bibb"
    title: "Error-Correction Coding for Digital Communications"
    publisher:
      name: "Plenum Press"
    year: 1981
    notes: >-
      Standard text covering block interleaving with row-wise write and
      column-wise read. Bibliographic details read from the Communications
      Blockset bibliography on 2026-10-06; no page number is quoted and no result
      is attributed to the text.
  - type: report
    authors:
      - family-names: Dolinar
        given-names: "S."
      - family-names: Divsalar
        given-names: "D."
    title: "Weight Distributions for Turbo Codes Using Random and Nonrandom Permutations"
    collection-title: "Interplanetary Network Progress Report"
    number: "42-122"
    start: 56
    end: 65
    year: 1995
    month: 8
    notes: >-
      Read in full on 2026-10-06. It compares random, nonrandom and semirandom
      permutations for turbo codes. It does NOT state the S-random selection rule,
      so it is cited here as context for semirandom permutations only. The rule
      implemented in src/interleavekit/srandom.py is written out in that module's
      docstring rather than attributed, because no primary source for it was
      verified during this build.
